{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRxuXAznRkdgCxNBBt3Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Writing up the change in ANSWER.md now."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":929,"cache_read_input_tokens":25877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":929},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c5bcb2c-62fd-4fcd-9aab-bb91e8cf1ee6","uuid":"0ae8b5b0-559d-4f05-9e1e-cf8bf793cee0","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:37:18.696Z","request_id":"req_011CgRxuWvsAJEyhQf42r9N7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRxuXAznRkdgCxNBBt3Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EABsYGYLS56pddJArEB7RV","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-lcUolS/ANSWER.md","content":"# HR ranges snapping to watch zones — fix\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: HR corridors are now emitted as\n\n```json\n\"hr\": {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": 68, \"end\": 75}\n```\n\ninstead of\n\n```json\n\"hr\": {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": 68, \"end\": 75}\n```\n\nIf a plan step has the same low and high (`hr_pct: [x, x]`), it still emits a\nsingle `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": x}`.\n\n## Why\n\nIn Intervals' `workout_doc`, a target range is written as `start`/`end`.\n`value` means a single **fixed** target. `value` + `end` is not a valid range,\nso Intervals stored it (which is why the readback looks the same as what we\nsent) but treated each step as one fixed HR number. When it exports a fixed HR\ntarget to Garmin, it widens the number to a band. That band comes from the\nzone/range settings, not from our plan. So the watch showed whole zone bands\n(118–137, 151–162, …), and the bands moved when the watch's LTHR changed.\n\nThe readback table supports this: in every step, the band the watch showed is\nthe watch zone that contains our planned **upper** bound (75 % → 129 → Z2,\n93 % → 160 → Z4, 82 % → 141 → Z3, 72 % → 124 → Z2). Our planned span never\nreached the watch.\n\nThings I deliberately did not do:\n- Absolute bpm (`units: \"bpm\"`): Garmin's FIT format stores custom HR targets\n  as bpm + 100, and earlier tries came out offset (214–249). Keeping `%lthr`\n  lets Intervals convert with LTHR 172 at export, as the README intends.\n- `start_bpm`/`end_bpm`: these aren't workout_doc fields. They were ignored,\n  which is where the constant 101 came from.\n\n## Not verified\n\nPer the ground rules, I didn't push anything to Intervals.icu, so I haven't\nconfirmed this on the watch. The field names come from Intervals'\n`workout_doc` conventions (the same `start`/`end` shape it uses for power and\npace ranges). They are not from an official schema I could check offline. To\nconfirm:\n\n```\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n./push.sh event.json\n```\n\nOn the watch, Tempo should then read about 151–160 and Float about 129–141.\nIf it still shows zone edges, check the athlete's Intervals Garmin/HR export\nsettings to see whether HR targets are being exported as zones.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":929,"cache_read_input_tokens":25877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":929},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c5bcb2c-62fd-4fcd-9aab-bb91e8cf1ee6","uuid":"0f203b20-3ec5-4e79-9701-dcb8d1975a1d","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:37:26.002Z","request_id":"req_011CgRxuWvsAJEyhQf42r9N7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EABsYGYLS56pddJArEB7RV":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-lcUolS/ANSWER.md","content":"# HR ranges snapping to watch zones — fix\n\n## What changed\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: HR corridors are now emitted as\n\n```json\n\"hr\": {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": 68, \"end\": 75}\n```\n\ninstead of\n\n```json\n\"hr\": {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": 68, \"end\": 75}\n```\n\nIf a plan step has the same low and high (`hr_pct: [x, x]`), it still emits a\nsingle `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": x}`.\n\n## Why\n\nIn Intervals' `workout_doc`, a target range is written as `start`/`end`.\n`value` means a single **fixed** target. `value` + `end` is not a valid range,\nso Intervals stored it (which is why the readback looks the same as what we\nsent) but treated each step as one fixed HR number. When it exports a fixed HR\ntarget to Garmin, it widens the number to a band. That band comes from the\nzone/range settings, not from our plan. So the watch showed whole zone bands\n(118–137, 151–162, …), and the bands moved when the watch's LTHR changed.\n\nThe readback table supports this: in every step, the band the watch showed is\nthe watch zone that contains our planned **upper** bound (75 % → 129 → Z2,\n93 % → 160 → Z4, 82 % → 141 → Z3, 72 % → 124 → Z2). Our planned span never\nreached the watch.\n\nThings I deliberately did not do:\n- Absolute bpm (`units: \"bpm\"`): Garmin's FIT format stores custom HR targets\n  as bpm + 100, and earlier tries came out offset (214–249). Keeping `%lthr`\n  lets Intervals convert with LTHR 172 at export, as the README intends.\n- `start_bpm`/`end_bpm`: these aren't workout_doc fields. They were ignored,\n  which is where the constant 101 came from.\n\n## Not verified\n\nPer the ground rules, I didn't push anything to Intervals.icu, so I haven't\nconfirmed this on the watch. The field names come from Intervals'\n`workout_doc` conventions (the same `start`/`end` shape it uses for power and\npace ranges). They are not from an official schema I could check offline. To\nconfirm:\n\n```\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n./push.sh event.json\n```\n\nOn the watch, Tempo should then read about 151–160 and Float about 129–141.\nIf it still shows zone edges, check the athlete's Intervals Garmin/HR export\nsettings to see whether HR targets are being exported as zones.\n"}}}
