ntends.\n- `start_bpm`/`end_bpm`: these aren't workout_doc fields. They were ignored,\n  which is where the constant 101 came from.\n\n## Not verified\n\nPer the ground rules, I didn't push anything to Intervals.icu, so I haven't\nconfirmed this on the watch. The field names come from Intervals'\n`workout_doc` conventions (the same `start`/`end` shape it uses for power and\npace ranges). They are not from an official schema I could check offline. To\nconfirm:\n\n```\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n./push.sh event.json\n```\n\nOn the watch, Tempo should then read about 151–160 and Float about 129–141.\nIf it still shows zone edges, check the athlete's Intervals Garmin/HR export\nsettings to see whether HR targets are being exported as zones.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EABsYGYLS56pddJArEB7RV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRxvGFb1ytfbWWseigQF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator so HR targets go out as real ranges, but I haven't checked the result on the watch: I didn't push anything to Intervals.\n\n**Cause:** `gen.py` wrote each HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. In Intervals' workout format, `value` means one fixed target; a range uses `start` and `end`. Intervals accepted and stored the body unchanged, but treated every step as a single HR number. When it sends a single number to Garmin, it widens it to a zone band, so the watch showed its own zone edges. Your readback table fits this: each band the watch showed is the zone that contains the step's planned upper limit.\n\n**Fix (`gen.py`, `hr_target`):** targets are now written as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. A step whose low and high are equal still gets a single `value`. Running the generator on the tempo plan produces the new shape. I kept the targets as % of LTHR so Intervals still converts them using LTHR 172.\n\nThat `start`/`end` is the right shape comes from how Intervals writes power and pace ranges, not from official docs. My web search only found forum threads, though they back the \"single target gets widened\" behaviour.\n\n**To confirm:**\n```\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json\n./push.sh event.json\n```\nTempo should then read about 151–160 and Float about 129–141 on the watch. If it still shows zone edges, check the athlete's Intervals settings for exporting HR targets to Garmin.\n\nANSWER.md also explains why the earlier bpm and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` tries went wrong.\n\nSources (community forum only):\n- [Building workout using zone number instead of percentage range](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/building-workout-using-zone-number-instead-of-percentage-range/4725?page=2)\n- [Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/upload-planned-workouts-to-garmin-connect/1521/157)\n- [Need help with HR-based workouts (in bpm)](https://forum.intervals.icu/t/need-help-with-hr-based-workouts-in-bpm/120685)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1035,"cache_read_input_tokens":26806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1035},"output_tokens":746,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c5bcb2c-62fd-4fcd-9aab-bb91e8cf1ee6","uuid":"61085734-ca22-4ae1-a14e-834e98c5a8cb","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:37:33.297Z","request_id":"req_011CgRxvG2wrQ3r2vJPiJXxg"}
